Questions:
Find Command Operations with Answers and Explanations
Basic Find Command Operations
Find files by name:
1. Find all .txt files in /var/log

bash
find /var/log -name "*.txt"
Explanation: -name matches the base filename against the pattern. Quotes prevent shell expansion.

2. Find files named hosts in the entire system

bash
find / -name hosts 2>/dev/null
Explanation: 2>/dev/null suppresses permission denied errors. Searches from root /.

3. Case-insensitive search for files named passwd

bash
find / -iname passwd 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -iname performs case-insensitive matching.

Find files by type:
4. Find all directories in /home

bash
find /home -type d
Explanation: -type d restricts search to directories only.

5. Find all symbolic links in /etc

bash
find /etc -type l
Explanation: -type l finds symbolic links.

6. Find all regular files in /tmp

bash
find /tmp -type f
Explanation: -type f finds regular files (not directories, links, etc.).

Find files by size:
7. Find files larger than 100MB in /var

bash
find /var -type f -size +100M
Explanation: -size +100M = larger than 100 megabytes. M = 1024×1024 bytes.

8. Find empty files in current directory

bash
find . -type f -empty
Explanation: -empty matches empty files and directories. -type f restricts to files.

9. Find files between 1MB and 10MB

bash
find / -type f -size +1M -size -10M 2>/dev/null
Explanation: Two -size tests combined: >1MB AND <10MB.

Find files by permissions:
10. Find files with SUID bit set

bash
find / -type f -perm /4000 2>/dev/null
# OR
find / -type f -perm -u=s 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -perm /4000 = any SUID bit set. / means "any of these bits". Octal 4000 = SUID.

11. Find world-writable files

bash
find / -type f -perm /002 2>/dev/null
# OR
find / -type f -perm /o=w 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -perm /002 = world-writable bit set. Security concern!

12. Find files owned by specific user

bash
find / -user jdoe 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -user jdoe matches files owned by user jdoe (name or UID).

Find files by time:
13. Find files modified in last 24 hours

bash
find / -type f -mtime 0
Explanation: -mtime 0 = modified within last 24 hours. 0 means "0 days ago".

14. Find files accessed more than 30 days ago

bash
find / -type f -atime +30
Explanation: -atime +30 = last accessed more than 30 days ago. + means "greater than".

15. Find files changed (status) in last 7 days

bash
find / -type f -ctime -7
Explanation: -ctime -7 = status changed within last 7 days. Status changes include permission, ownership changes. - means "less than".

Common RHCSA-Style Scenarios
File management tasks:
16. Find and delete all .tmp files in /tmp

bash
find /tmp -type f -name "*.tmp" -delete
# Safer alternative:
find /tmp -type f -name "*.tmp" -exec rm -f {} \;
Explanation: -delete removes files. Always test first with -print instead of -delete.

17. Find and compress all log files older than 7 days

bash
find /var/log -name "*.log" -mtime +7 -exec gzip {} \;
Explanation: -exec gzip {} \; runs gzip on each file. {} is replaced by filename.

18. Find and change permissions of files owned by a specific user

bash
find /home -user jdoe -type f -exec chmod 644 {} \;
Explanation: Changes permissions to 644 (rw-r--r--) for all jdoe's regular files.

System administration tasks:
19. Find all files modified after a certain date for audit

bash
touch -t 202312010000 /tmp/timestamp
find / -type f -newer /tmp/timestamp 2>/dev/null
Explanation: Creates a reference file with specific timestamp, finds files newer than it.

20. Locate configuration files that have been changed

bash
find /etc -type f -name "*.conf" -newer /etc/passwd
Explanation: Finds .conf files in /etc modified more recently than /etc/passwd.

21. Find core dump files and remove them

bash
find / -type f -name "core" -o -name "core.*" -delete 2>/dev/null
# OR safer:
find / -type f \( -name "core" -o -name "core.*" \) -exec rm -f {} \; 2>/dev/null
Explanation: \( ... \) groups conditions. -o = OR. Finds files named "core" or "core.*".

